# Rank 1962 / CVE-2026-0933: wrangler's `pages deploy` interpolates the
# `--commit-hash` flag into a shell command. The command handler is an object
# method (`async handler(args)`) inside the object literal handed to
# createCommand, and the flag value arrives through it:
#
#   let { branch, commitHash, commitMessage, commitDirty } = args;
#   ...
#   commitMessage = execSync(`git show -s --format=%B ${commitHash}`)
#       .toString()
#       .trim();
#
# Whoever controls the CLI flag controls a fragment of a command string that
# execSync hands to a shell (CWE-78). The fixed revision, 99b1f328, passes the value as
# one argv element instead: execFileSync("git", ["show", "-s", "--format=%B",
# commitHash]), no shell in between.
#
# Three things had to come together, and the spec pins each:
#
# 1. Vocabulary (shipped here): node:child_process documents execSync as
#    spawning a shell over ONE command string while execFileSync/spawn take an
#    argv array, so "an execSync spelling plus a template-built string" is the
#    review shape code.CommandStringWrapperExecution / VYQL-INJ-119
#    judges. jsExecSyncFormattedCommandString
#    (bindings/javascript/command_string_template_execution.vyql) widens that
#    family to the interpolated-template spelling; the identifier spelling
#    (command/cmd locals) commandStringWrapperExecution251 covers
#    stays as is. The negation on the execFile/spawn family follows the
#    documented remediation and the existing binding's shape.
# 2. Extraction (method shorthand): a method defined inside an object literal
#    that is directly a call argument is never lowered when the call is a
#    declarator's value — the arrow-function spelling of the same property does.
#    The whole 265-line handler produced no nodes and no function-context
#    tokens, so nothing could match. Case B pins the miss; it flips to expect
#    with the callArgObjectMethodFuncDefs fix.
# 3. Lowering (try/catch constants): the handler probes for a git repo with
#    try { execSync("git rev-parse ...") } catch { isGitDir = false; } and
#    guards everything after with if (isGitDir). The catch's assignment is
#    const-folded and the constant leaks out of the try's control regions, so
#    opaque-predicate pruning read if (isGitDir) as dead and dropped the
#    guarded statements — including the vulnerable call — from the graph.
#    Case E pins the miss through the working process.argv source; it flips
#    to expect with the nir.Try const-join fix.
#
# The presence route (VYQL-INJ-119) reads the function's AST-derived context
# tokens, so it fires without the lowering fix once the handler is extracted;
# the taint route (VYQL-INJ-002) needs the sink node and is what case E pins.
# The handler parameter itself carries no source label — the argv dispatch is
# framework wiring no documented API exposes in-file — which is why the
# taint route stays dark on cases A-D and the review rule carries detection.
#
# verify_current_expected: A, C and E's INJ-119 arm expect; B rejects until
# the extraction fix lands, E's INJ-002 arm rejects until the lowering fix
# lands; D (the fixed revision) rejects both rules.

test "rank1962: an interpolated commit hash reaches execSync's shell command string"
  lang javascript
  file deploy.ts
  expect VYQL-INJ-119
  code
  ```
  import { execSync } from "node:child_process";

  export const pagesDeployCommand = createCommand({
  	handler: async (args) => {
  		const { commitHash, commitMessage } = args;

  		if (!commitMessage) {
  			commitMessage = execSync(`git show -s --format=%B ${commitHash}`)
  				.toString()
  				.trim();
  		}
  	},
  });
  ```

test "rank1962: the same handler written as an object method inside the createCommand argument"
  lang javascript
  file deploy.ts
  expect VYQL-INJ-119
  code
  ```
  import { execSync } from "node:child_process";

  export const pagesDeployCommand = createCommand({
